## File Organization Examples

### ✅ CORRECT Placement

```
development_guidelines/
├── README.md (root - allowed, essential reference)
├── HOW_TO_RUN.md (root - allowed, essential reference)
├── learnings/
│   ├── testflight_crash_fix_missing_env_vars.md ✅
│   ├── EAS_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLES_GUIDE.md ✅
│   └── 2025-11-11_modal-rendering-bug.md ✅
├── planned/
│   ├── 01_component_library.md ✅
│   └── paywall_implementation.md ✅
├── delivered/
│   ├── onboarding_implementation.md ✅
│   └── PRE_FLIGHT_CHECKLIST.md ✅
└── running/
    └── feature-name_wip.md ✅
```

### ❌ INCORRECT Placement

```
project-root/
├── TROUBLESHOOTING.md ❌ (should be in development_guidelines/learnings/)
├── FEATURE_PLAN.md ❌ (should be in development_guidelines/planned/)
└── development_guidelines/
    ├── random-doc.md ❌ (should be in a subdirectory)
    └── learnings/
        └── ...
```

---

## Enforcement

**This rule applies to:**
- All new documentation creation
- All documentation updates that involve moving or creating files
- All AI-generated documentation
- All manual documentation

**Exceptions:**
- `README.md` in project root (standard practice)
- `CLAUDE.md` in project root (project guide)
- Essential reference files in `development_guidelines/` root (as specified)

**When in doubt:**
1. Check `development_guidelines/README.md` for guidance
2. Look at existing documentation structure
3. Choose the most appropriate subdirectory
4. If truly essential quick reference, ask before placing in root

